[38;2;124;124;124mLet me solve this. The system is:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124mx₅ + x₂ = yx₁[0m
[38;2;124;124;124mx₁ + x₃ = yx₂[0m
[38;2;124;124;124mx₂ + x₄ = yx₃[0m
[38;2;124;124;124mx₃ + x₅ = yx₄[0m
[38;2;124;124;124mx₄ + x₁ = yx₅[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThis is a circulant system. The matrix is circulant. Let me write it as:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124my x₁ - x₂ - x₅ = 0[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m-x₁ + y x₂ - x₃ = 0[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m-x₂ + y x₃ - x₄ = 0[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m-x₃ + y x₄ - x₅ = 0[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m-x₁ - x₄ + y x₅ = 0[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe matrix M = yI - (shift matrix). Actually it's a circulant matrix with first [0m[38;2;124;124;124mrow [y, -1, 0, 0, -1].[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe eigenvalues of a circulant matrix with first row [c0, c1, c2, c3, c4] are [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mλ_k = c0 + c1 ω^k + c2 ω^{2k} + c3 ω^{3k} + c4 ω^{4k} where ω = e^{2πi/5}.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mHere c0 = y, c1 = -1, c2 = 0, c3 = 0, c4 = -1.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo λ_k = y - ω^k - ω^{4k} = y - (ω^k + ω^{-k}) = y - 2cos(2πk/5).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mFor k=0: λ_0 = y - 2.[0m
[38;2;124;124;124mFor k=1: λ_1 = y - 2cos(2π/5) = y - 2·(√5-1)/4 = y - (√5-1)/2.[0m
[38;2;124;124;124mFor k=2: λ_2 = y - 2cos(4π/5) = y - 2·(-(√5+1)/4) = y + (√5+1)/2.[0m
[38;2;124;124;124mFor k=3: λ_3 = λ_2 (since cos(6π/5)=cos(4π/5)).[0m
[38;2;124;124;124mFor k=4: λ_4 = λ_1.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo the determinant is the product of eigenvalues:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124mdet = (y-2)(y - (√5-1)/2)²(y + (√5+1)/2)².[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe system has nontrivial solutions iff det = 0.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo the values of y for which nontrivial solutions exist are:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- y = 2[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- y = (√5-1)/2[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- y = -(√5+1)/2[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mFor all other values of y, the only solution is x₁=x₂=x₃=x₄=x₅=0.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mNow let me find the solutions for each case.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m**Case 1: y = 2.** λ_0 = 0, so the eigenvector is (1,1,1,1,1). All other [0m
[38;2;124;124;124meigenvalues nonzero. So solution space is 1-dimensional: x₁=x₂=x₃=x₄=x₅ = t for [0m[38;2;124;124;124many t.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m**Case 2: y = (√5-1)/2.** λ_1 = λ_4 = 0. Two-dimensional solution space. The [0m
[38;2;124;124;124meigenvectors for k=1 and k=4 are complex conjugates. Real solutions are linear [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mcombinations.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe eigenvector for k=1 is (1, ω, ω², ω³, ω⁴) where ω = e^{2πi/5}. Taking real [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mand imaginary parts:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124mReal part: (1, cos(2π/5), cos(4π/5), cos(6π/5), cos(8π/5)) = (1, cos(2π/5), [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mcos(4π/5), cos(4π/5), cos(2π/5))[0m
[38;2;124;124;124mImaginary part: (0, sin(2π/5), sin(4π/5), sin(6π/5), sin(8π/5)) = (0, sin(2π/5),[0m[38;2;124;124;124msin(4π/5), -sin(4π/5), -sin(2π/5))[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo the real solution space is spanned by:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124mv₁ = (1, cos(2π/5), cos(4π/5), cos(4π/5), cos(2π/5))[0m
[38;2;124;124;124mv₂ = (0, sin(2π/5), sin(4π/5), -sin(4π/5), -sin(2π/5))[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mNote cos(2π/5) = (√5-1)/4, cos(4π/5) = -(√5+1)/4, sin(2π/5) = √(10+2√5)/4, [0m
[38;2;124;124;124msin(4π/5) = √(10-2√5)/4.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m**Case 3: y = -(√5+1)/2.** λ_2 = λ_3 = 0. Two-dimensional solution space. [0m
